[2026-09-21 14:30:46 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 010] --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
[2026-09-21 14:30:46 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 010]   TEP / Planet-9 -- step 02: the perihelion-clustering anomaly
[2026-09-21 14:30:46 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 010]   Reproduces, from the full JPL SBDB catalog, the anomalous clustering
[2026-09-21 14:30:46 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 010]   of distant trans-Neptunian object orbits that motivates the Planet-9
[2026-09-21 14:30:46 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 010]   hypothesis (Trujillo & Sheppard 2014; Batygin & Brown 2016;
[2026-09-21 14:30:46 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 010]   Brown & Batygin 2021).
[2026-09-21 14:30:46 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 010]   Under the standard interpretation the clustering is shepherding by an
[2026-09-21 14:30:46 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 010]   unseen point mass.  Under TEP the clustered perihelion longitudes are
[2026-09-21 14:30:46 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 010]   orbital clocks phase-locked about a fixed inertial direction -- the
[2026-09-21 14:30:46 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 010]   signature of a spatially structured proper-time field, not a body.
[2026-09-21 14:30:46 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 010]   Method -- a *conditioned* null.  Each orbit is decomposed into
[2026-09-21 14:30:46 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 010]   geometric elements (a, e, i: shape and plane tilt) and clock elements
[2026-09-21 14:30:46 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 010]   (node om, argument of perihelion w, and varpi = om + w).  The null
[2026-09-21 14:30:46 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 010]   hypothesis holds every object's observed (a, e, i) fixed and draws om,
[2026-09-21 14:30:46 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 010]   w uniform on the circle.  Clustering is therefore tested in the
[2026-09-21 14:30:46 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 010]   angular/clock sector only, conditioned on the observed geometric
[2026-09-21 14:30:46 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 010]   sector -- precisely where a temporal-field boundary acts, and free of
[2026-09-21 14:30:46 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 010]   the inclination-marginal bias that afflicts naive pole-vector tests.
[2026-09-21 14:30:46 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 010]   Sample definitions follow the literature convention:
[2026-09-21 14:30:46 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 010]   detached:    a > 150 AU, q > 30 AU
[2026-09-21 14:30:46 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 010]   extreme:     a > 250 AU, q > 30 AU
[2026-09-21 14:30:46 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 010]   sednoid:     a > 150 AU, q > 50 AU
[2026-09-21 14:30:46 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 010]   Orbit quality: primary results use condition_code <= 3 (secure,
[2026-09-21 14:30:46 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 010]   multi-opposition orbits), as is standard in this literature; the
[2026-09-21 14:30:46 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 010]   unfiltered catalog is shown for contrast.
[2026-09-21 14:30:46 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 010]   Statistics per sample: Rayleigh R of varpi; spherical resultant R of
[2026-09-21 14:30:46 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 010]   the perihelion unit vectors phat; spherical R of the folded orbit
[2026-09-21 14:30:46 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 010]   poles nhat; each calibrated against 20000 conditioned Monte-Carlo
[2026-09-21 14:30:46 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 010]   realizations.  Bootstrap 68% intervals on the mean direction.
[2026-09-21 14:30:46 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 010]   Outputs: results/step_02_clustering.json,
[2026-09-21 14:30:46 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 010]   figures/supplementary/step_02_clustering.png
[2026-09-21 14:30:46 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 010] --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
